1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,160
And now, to you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:10,840
Here's a little conundrum for you.

3
00:00:11,580 --> 00:00:17,540
I don't know why this is, but it seems to be true of all the scouts that I've ever had

4
00:00:17,540 --> 00:00:20,620
as a member of the troop over the many, many years.

5
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:27,260
They all seem to think that a nylon tent is a soundproof booth.

6
00:00:27,260 --> 00:00:28,840
Do you know what I'm saying?

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,880
If you don't, next time you're camping and the scouts go off to their tents for the night,

8
00:00:34,960 --> 00:00:36,020
just walk around a little bit.

9
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:37,940
Yeah.

10
00:00:38,400 --> 00:00:40,260
They all think that it's a soundproof booth.

11
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:41,560
Hmm.

12
00:00:43,300 --> 00:00:44,040
Hey, listen.

13
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:45,940
This is podcast number 18.

14
00:00:45,940 --> 00:01:06,000
Hey!

15
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:22,020
Hi, this is Clark Green.

16
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,720
Welcome back to the Scoutmasters Podcast.

17
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:24,680
Glad to have you.

18
00:01:25,240 --> 00:01:31,460
Now listen, last time around, I promised that we would have an interview with the author

19
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:34,900
of a great new book of scouting history called The Scouting Party.

20
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:43,120
And I did get on Skype with the author and we talked for a while and then I found out

21
00:01:43,120 --> 00:01:46,560
that somehow I had screwed up recording the conversation.

22
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:51,880
So unfortunately, we're going to have to wait a little while for that.

23
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,660
Hopefully, maybe the next podcast because it's a great book and I really want you to hear

24
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:57,380
about it.

25
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,620
So, but that's all right.

26
00:02:00,220 --> 00:02:00,740
Guess what?

27
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:02,380
I can still talk.

28
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:07,140
Yes, sir.

29
00:02:07,260 --> 00:02:10,860
So we put together a couple other things for the podcast today.

30
00:02:11,620 --> 00:02:16,760
The first thing you'll hear is the 10 best moments in scouting from my perspective.

31
00:02:17,420 --> 00:02:22,980
And then we're going to have another email answer, an email that came in recently.

32
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,220
And that'll be about it for today.

33
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:32,940
Oh, we might sneak in another episode of Brick Mason, that historic Scoutmaster show I told

34
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:33,280
you about.

35
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:35,300
Yeah.

36
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:40,000
So, but that should be planning for a podcast, don't you think?

37
00:02:40,600 --> 00:02:41,200
Well, of course.

38
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:42,340
So let's get started.

39
00:02:42,340 --> 00:03:06,120
You know, as the podcast goes and the blog goes, sometimes we're dealing with problems

40
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:07,920
and it seems like we're dealing with problems all the time.

41
00:03:07,920 --> 00:03:13,839
You know, people email me about problems and difficulties they're having and, you know,

42
00:03:13,839 --> 00:03:19,459
you just run into situations where you're trying to make all the puzzle pieces of scouting

43
00:03:19,459 --> 00:03:21,060
fit together and make things work.

44
00:03:21,679 --> 00:03:24,679
And there's challenges and difficulties, but there's also great times.

45
00:03:25,679 --> 00:03:28,540
There's also wonderful, great moments in scouting.

46
00:03:28,540 --> 00:03:35,340
So in no particular order, my 10 best, the 10 best moments I've had as a Scoutmaster.

47
00:03:36,880 --> 00:03:45,880
Number one, there was one early spring night, we were out camping and we were staying in

48
00:03:45,880 --> 00:03:47,120
a cabin at our Scout camp.

49
00:03:48,160 --> 00:03:54,820
And one of the meteor showers was going to happen that Saturday night.

50
00:03:54,820 --> 00:04:00,480
So we all got up at three in the morning on Saturday morning and we hiked our sleeping

51
00:04:00,480 --> 00:04:06,060
bags and our sleeping pads down into a field at camp and we watched this meteor shower.

52
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,140
We just laid there on our backs and watched the meteor shower.

53
00:04:10,799 --> 00:04:13,000
It was damp and it was cold.

54
00:04:13,940 --> 00:04:15,340
I mean, it was seriously cold.

55
00:04:16,700 --> 00:04:21,659
But the stars were showing through the skies as if they were close enough to touch.

56
00:04:21,659 --> 00:04:23,600
You could just about reach up and grab one.

57
00:04:24,820 --> 00:04:31,719
And within an hour, we had counted over 100 shooting stars.

58
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:36,900
And I think when we hit 100, we thought, well, that's probably enough for anybody.

59
00:04:38,360 --> 00:04:40,640
And we escaped back to the warmth of our cabin.

60
00:04:41,300 --> 00:04:51,980
Number two, had a Scout who crossed over as a weebleos and joined the troop very shortly

61
00:04:51,980 --> 00:04:53,900
after his father had passed away suddenly.

62
00:04:53,900 --> 00:05:02,260
And his father was an Eagle Scout and he was his son's cub master.

63
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:06,220
And it was a very sad time for his family.

64
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:08,080
It was horrible.

65
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:14,760
But the boy came over to the troop and we worked through things over years.

66
00:05:14,760 --> 00:05:17,900
And he went on to become an Eagle Scout.

67
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:21,400
And one of my favorite Scouts.

68
00:05:22,219 --> 00:05:23,360
Don't tell anybody.

69
00:05:23,820 --> 00:05:25,200
But one of my favorite Scouts.

70
00:05:26,740 --> 00:05:33,840
And at his Eagle Court of Honor, as he was saying a few words, as all the Eagle Scouts do,

71
00:05:34,840 --> 00:05:37,420
he presented me with his Eagle Mentor pin.

72
00:05:37,420 --> 00:05:42,940
And that's, that was a great moment.

73
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:48,400
Then number three, also an Eagle moment.

74
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:50,360
And these are in no particular order.

75
00:05:51,320 --> 00:05:56,580
Was watching my wife pin an Eagle medal on my stepson's uniform.

76
00:05:57,700 --> 00:05:58,900
That was quite a moment.

77
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:06,239
Number four, canoeing around the corner of a lake shoreline in Canada.

78
00:06:07,700 --> 00:06:09,400
Now, here it is.

79
00:06:09,460 --> 00:06:10,599
It's one early morning.

80
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:13,960
And it had rained the night before.

81
00:06:14,159 --> 00:06:16,780
And it looked like it was going to rain pretty much of that day.

82
00:06:17,099 --> 00:06:20,580
But we had this one clear time early in the morning where we could get out.

83
00:06:20,979 --> 00:06:22,219
There wasn't going to be a lot of wind.

84
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:26,219
We could kind of shoot across this big lake before the waves came up.

85
00:06:26,220 --> 00:06:32,280
And we could get to a portage that would continue our trip.

86
00:06:33,120 --> 00:06:36,200
And so we were up and packed and in the canoes.

87
00:06:36,440 --> 00:06:38,380
And there's a bit of a mist over the water.

88
00:06:39,320 --> 00:06:41,220
And we're canoeing along.

89
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:46,860
And we come across the shoreline and go past just a little tiny inlet in the shoreline.

90
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:52,580
And there's a moose in all his glory standing there watching us go past.

91
00:06:52,580 --> 00:06:56,219
I'll always have that picture in my mind.

92
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:57,099
That was a great moment.

93
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:06,719
Number five, we have an annual father and son weekend every year.

94
00:07:07,380 --> 00:07:09,280
And we get a big turnout for it.

95
00:07:10,219 --> 00:07:12,260
And we plan it well in advance.

96
00:07:12,360 --> 00:07:13,860
I mean, it's planned a year in advance.

97
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:23,500
And somewhere within the course of that year, I found out that I was supposed to attend our council dinner to be recognized with the Silver Beaver Award.

98
00:07:24,460 --> 00:07:28,180
And it was cross-scheduled with our father and son weekend.

99
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:31,720
And I think I knew where my duties lay.

100
00:07:32,100 --> 00:07:34,980
And it was with the troop at the father and son weekend.

101
00:07:34,980 --> 00:07:38,580
And so I had to politely decline attending the council dinner.

102
00:07:39,840 --> 00:07:46,560
So the next night, which was a Saturday night at the father and son weekend, some of my scouting buddies came down.

103
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:52,820
And they presented my Silver Beaver Award at the father and son weekend with my scouts.

104
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,780
And that was an incredible moment.

105
00:07:57,140 --> 00:07:58,060
That was just great.

106
00:07:58,060 --> 00:08:03,900
And to have all my scouts there as that happened was very memorable.

107
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:16,680
Number six, whenever I see one of my old scouts, when they take the time to stop in and say hello when they're in town, that is a great moment.

108
00:08:17,180 --> 00:08:26,720
Whether they've been away for 10 months or 10 years, it's always good to see them come back and to remember the time that they had in scouting.

109
00:08:28,060 --> 00:08:38,200
Number seven, anytime I get to shake hands with a new scout as he steps off the end of a ceremonial bridge at a Weevilow's bridge crossing ceremony,

110
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:43,799
and the knowledge that we'll likely shake hands over an eagle badge someday, that's a great moment.

111
00:08:45,400 --> 00:08:48,720
Number eight, as a member of the Order of the Arrow,

112
00:08:49,840 --> 00:08:55,760
keeping my vigil throughout a really wonderfully serene spring moonlit night

113
00:08:55,760 --> 00:08:59,800
on a hillside in one of my favorite places in the world, our scout camp,

114
00:09:00,280 --> 00:09:04,460
and hearing the voices of so many good friends supporting me through my vigil.

115
00:09:05,460 --> 00:09:07,540
I will always remember that night.

116
00:09:08,920 --> 00:09:09,800
Number nine

117
00:09:09,800 --> 00:09:13,140
is the

118
00:09:13,140 --> 00:09:15,660
real pleasure I get

119
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:18,439
whenever I am able to introduce

120
00:09:18,439 --> 00:09:19,800
an evil candidate

121
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:22,240
to the members of his border review.

122
00:09:22,240 --> 00:09:24,720
that's always a good moment.

123
00:09:26,020 --> 00:09:27,020
And number 10,

124
00:09:27,980 --> 00:09:29,160
and this happens a lot,

125
00:09:30,040 --> 00:09:31,720
and it's one of the most satisfying

126
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:33,040
things

127
00:09:33,040 --> 00:09:34,800
about being a scoutmaster,

128
00:09:35,320 --> 00:09:38,180
and that is listening to the excited voices and laughter

129
00:09:38,180 --> 00:09:40,640
of a group of scouts as they head off

130
00:09:40,640 --> 00:09:41,740
somewhere to do something.

131
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:46,040
Whether we're on a camp out

132
00:09:46,040 --> 00:09:47,020
and they're going on a hike,

133
00:09:47,460 --> 00:09:49,960
whether they're headed off to make their dinner or something like that,

134
00:09:49,960 --> 00:09:51,960
just listening to them.

135
00:09:52,920 --> 00:09:54,380
Listening to them being excited

136
00:09:54,380 --> 00:09:55,260
and interested

137
00:09:55,260 --> 00:09:56,780
and having a great time in scouts.

138
00:09:57,480 --> 00:09:59,240
That's all that you really ever need.

139
00:09:59,540 --> 00:10:00,620
So there's my 10 best.

140
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,400
I'd like to hear about your best moments in scouting.

141
00:10:04,300 --> 00:10:06,540
Send them to me here at the Scoutmasters podcast.

142
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:36,520
The Scoutmasters podcast.

143
00:10:36,540 --> 00:10:39,439
Send it by mail.

144
00:10:40,959 --> 00:10:42,560
Email that is, folks.

145
00:10:43,099 --> 00:10:46,160
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

146
00:10:55,839 --> 00:10:58,380
Scoutmaster Chuck wrote me by email,

147
00:10:58,740 --> 00:10:59,900
and he says this,

148
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:02,380
I've recently taken over as Scoutmaster,

149
00:11:02,520 --> 00:11:04,760
and our troop needs to reorganize its patrols.

150
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,480
We have 38 scouts and 3 patrols.

151
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:08,140
Good for you.

152
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:10,140
38 scouts is a good, healthy troop.

153
00:11:10,720 --> 00:11:12,800
One has 11 or 12 scouts, all young,

154
00:11:12,860 --> 00:11:14,360
and most of them go camping.

155
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:17,340
The second has about 6 active scouts,

156
00:11:17,640 --> 00:11:19,680
2 of which camp regularly.

157
00:11:20,340 --> 00:11:22,120
Patrol 3 is led by a life scout

158
00:11:22,120 --> 00:11:23,819
that is great with younger scouts,

159
00:11:23,939 --> 00:11:25,300
but is kind of too cool

160
00:11:25,300 --> 00:11:26,620
even to wear his uniform.

161
00:11:27,319 --> 00:11:28,340
The senior patrol leader

162
00:11:28,340 --> 00:11:29,980
and assistant senior patrol leader

163
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:33,600
are national youth leader training trained,

164
00:11:33,840 --> 00:11:34,980
and they're very motivated.

165
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:37,640
I think we're moving towards maybe 5 patrols,

166
00:11:37,700 --> 00:11:38,680
one new scout patrol,

167
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:39,720
one venture patrol,

168
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:41,460
three regular patrols.

169
00:11:42,060 --> 00:11:43,400
The Patrol Leaders Council

170
00:11:43,400 --> 00:11:44,960
is going to define the requirements

171
00:11:44,960 --> 00:11:46,720
to get into the new venture patrol.

172
00:11:47,320 --> 00:11:49,460
I'm guessing it might be the boys

173
00:11:49,460 --> 00:11:50,740
who are 15 and older.

174
00:11:51,260 --> 00:11:52,340
And the new scout patrol,

175
00:11:53,080 --> 00:11:54,220
by definition, is easy.

176
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:55,760
My concern is creating

177
00:11:55,760 --> 00:11:57,700
the three regular patrols.

178
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:00,020
How'd you go about having the scouts do this?

179
00:12:00,520 --> 00:12:01,980
Any help is much appreciated.

180
00:12:02,120 --> 00:12:02,640
Well, Chuck,

181
00:12:03,900 --> 00:12:04,940
first of all,

182
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:06,380
thanks for getting in touch,

183
00:12:06,680 --> 00:12:08,760
and congratulations on your new post

184
00:12:08,760 --> 00:12:09,640
as a scoutmaster.

185
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:12,620
I've got a troop that's a similar size.

186
00:12:12,700 --> 00:12:13,860
We're a little more than that right now.

187
00:12:13,900 --> 00:12:14,900
We're running around 45,

188
00:12:15,180 --> 00:12:17,560
and we have four active patrols

189
00:12:17,560 --> 00:12:18,360
and a venture patrol.

190
00:12:19,280 --> 00:12:22,160
I've used this new scout patrol

191
00:12:22,160 --> 00:12:24,000
with a couple of modifications.

192
00:12:24,980 --> 00:12:27,220
I'm going to get on a little bit of a rant here.

193
00:12:27,400 --> 00:12:28,580
The new scout patrol thing

194
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:31,580
where you bring the boys into the troop

195
00:12:31,580 --> 00:12:34,120
and they are in this kind of practice patrol

196
00:12:34,120 --> 00:12:36,340
that's going to dissolve in a while,

197
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:39,080
that smacks to me of being,

198
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:39,740
you know,

199
00:12:39,800 --> 00:12:42,000
the third year of Weeblos in a lot of ways.

200
00:12:42,780 --> 00:12:44,340
When the scouts come in,

201
00:12:44,480 --> 00:12:46,820
they might stay with the same boys

202
00:12:46,820 --> 00:12:48,720
that they were in Weeblos with,

203
00:12:48,740 --> 00:12:50,740
and they might be the same age group

204
00:12:50,740 --> 00:12:51,200
and everything,

205
00:12:51,740 --> 00:12:53,220
but they're in a real patrol.

206
00:12:53,220 --> 00:12:56,820
They're not in an artificially formed one

207
00:12:56,820 --> 00:12:58,260
that is going to disappear

208
00:12:58,260 --> 00:13:00,180
once they become first class

209
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:01,180
or something like that.

210
00:13:02,180 --> 00:13:05,400
So I use the new scout patrol thing

211
00:13:05,400 --> 00:13:06,880
with that modification.

212
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:12,440
We usually end up resetting our patrols

213
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:14,080
or reorganizing our patrols.

214
00:13:14,300 --> 00:13:16,660
It seems like maybe about every 18 months,

215
00:13:16,820 --> 00:13:19,440
maybe as often as once a year,

216
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:21,900
but, you know,

217
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:23,300
it doesn't seem to be,

218
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:27,260
it's not necessarily an annual exercise.

219
00:13:28,420 --> 00:13:29,920
The senior patrol leader

220
00:13:29,920 --> 00:13:31,320
and the patrol leader's council,

221
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:33,380
what they will do is they will sit down

222
00:13:33,380 --> 00:13:35,440
and they'll figure out how to do this.

223
00:13:35,500 --> 00:13:37,240
Now, one way that they've done it in the past

224
00:13:37,240 --> 00:13:39,920
is to get the scouts to write the names

225
00:13:39,920 --> 00:13:41,200
of five or six other scouts

226
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,620
they'd like to have in their patrol,

227
00:13:42,620 --> 00:13:44,780
and then they sit down

228
00:13:44,780 --> 00:13:45,860
and kind of chart it out

229
00:13:45,860 --> 00:13:49,060
and figure out which guys

230
00:13:49,060 --> 00:13:51,120
want to be in a patrol with which guys,

231
00:13:51,340 --> 00:13:53,060
and that usually works out.

232
00:13:53,360 --> 00:13:54,700
And I leave that totally up

233
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:55,760
to the senior patrol leader

234
00:13:55,760 --> 00:13:57,700
and his patrol leader's council

235
00:13:57,700 --> 00:14:00,960
because they are usually a better judge

236
00:14:00,960 --> 00:14:03,760
than I of who gets on with who

237
00:14:03,760 --> 00:14:05,700
and the hundred other considerations

238
00:14:05,700 --> 00:14:07,960
that go into making a decision like that.

239
00:14:07,960 --> 00:14:11,000
I remember having any reason,

240
00:14:11,180 --> 00:14:12,480
I can't remember,

241
00:14:12,980 --> 00:14:14,140
really having any reason

242
00:14:14,140 --> 00:14:15,840
to second guess them on those decisions.

243
00:14:16,020 --> 00:14:16,860
It's pretty remarkable

244
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:18,980
that they usually get it better

245
00:14:18,980 --> 00:14:20,840
and more right than I would.

246
00:14:21,600 --> 00:14:22,660
Our venture crew

247
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:26,140
is the senior patrol leader,

248
00:14:26,220 --> 00:14:27,640
the quartermaster to the scribe,

249
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:29,460
the assistant senior patrol leaders,

250
00:14:29,580 --> 00:14:31,320
and any junior assistant scout masters

251
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:32,500
that we have at the time.

252
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,240
Other than that, the SPL,

253
00:14:35,240 --> 00:14:39,240
well, other than the senior patrol leader

254
00:14:39,240 --> 00:14:41,140
and his fellow senior leaders,

255
00:14:41,720 --> 00:14:43,160
the scouts stay in their patrols.

256
00:14:43,860 --> 00:14:46,600
Now, we used to kind of move them up

257
00:14:46,600 --> 00:14:47,840
and out of patrols

258
00:14:47,840 --> 00:14:49,500
when they had reached 14 or 15

259
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:51,500
with the idea that they should get out of the way

260
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:52,640
and let the younger scouts

261
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:54,220
take over the patrol leader position.

262
00:14:54,740 --> 00:14:56,220
But over the past several years,

263
00:14:56,280 --> 00:14:57,200
we've stopped doing that

264
00:14:57,200 --> 00:14:59,020
so the patrols have a better mix

265
00:14:59,020 --> 00:15:00,340
of experience and ages.

266
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:01,900
And so far, it's so good.

267
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:03,280
I mean, you'd think that the older guys

268
00:15:03,280 --> 00:15:04,960
would complain about having to associate

269
00:15:04,960 --> 00:15:06,040
with the younger guys,

270
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:07,620
but they don't

271
00:15:07,620 --> 00:15:09,180
because there's always at least

272
00:15:09,180 --> 00:15:10,340
one other older scout

273
00:15:10,340 --> 00:15:11,320
in the patrol with them.

274
00:15:11,920 --> 00:15:13,660
The other important aspect of this

275
00:15:13,660 --> 00:15:14,700
is that the older scouts

276
00:15:14,700 --> 00:15:16,000
are responsible for planning

277
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:18,800
and presenting everything that goes on,

278
00:15:19,080 --> 00:15:20,440
whether they're in a patrol

279
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:23,840
or whether they have a senior patrol

280
00:15:23,840 --> 00:15:26,340
or a senior leadership position.

281
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:28,280
And this keeps them very busy

282
00:15:28,280 --> 00:15:29,020
and engaged

283
00:15:29,020 --> 00:15:30,760
and most of the time very happy.

284
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:33,939
My experience tells me

285
00:15:33,939 --> 00:15:35,439
that a patrol functions best,

286
00:15:35,560 --> 00:15:37,200
just like Baden-Powell said

287
00:15:37,200 --> 00:15:38,140
all those years ago,

288
00:15:38,240 --> 00:15:39,960
with a minimum of about six scouts

289
00:15:39,960 --> 00:15:41,800
and a maximum of maybe eight or nine.

290
00:15:42,580 --> 00:15:43,300
Any fewer,

291
00:15:43,819 --> 00:15:45,620
and you're skating

292
00:15:45,620 --> 00:15:48,340
because at any given outing

293
00:15:48,340 --> 00:15:49,880
or patrol meeting

294
00:15:49,880 --> 00:15:50,580
or troop meeting,

295
00:15:50,960 --> 00:15:51,980
there's only liable to be

296
00:15:51,980 --> 00:15:52,780
two or three guys

297
00:15:52,780 --> 00:15:55,100
if you have a patrol of six.

298
00:15:55,100 --> 00:15:57,040
If you have a patrol of eight or nine,

299
00:15:57,220 --> 00:15:58,660
you'll get four or five.

300
00:15:59,900 --> 00:16:02,340
Any more than eight or nine

301
00:16:02,340 --> 00:16:03,720
and things seem to be

302
00:16:03,720 --> 00:16:05,320
kind of unmanageable and chaotic.

303
00:16:05,560 --> 00:16:07,960
So I would point at that number.

304
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:11,140
You know, he said,

305
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:13,120
Chuck said that his senior patrol leader

306
00:16:13,120 --> 00:16:14,560
and assistant senior patrol leader

307
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:15,560
are really pumped up.

308
00:16:16,460 --> 00:16:18,500
And if I were in your shoes,

309
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:19,340
I would just go

310
00:16:19,340 --> 00:16:20,280
and I would ask them

311
00:16:20,280 --> 00:16:21,540
to handle the situation.

312
00:16:22,240 --> 00:16:22,820
Just say, hey,

313
00:16:22,820 --> 00:16:23,340
you know,

314
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:26,040
we probably should think about

315
00:16:26,040 --> 00:16:27,620
dividing the troop

316
00:16:27,620 --> 00:16:28,900
into new patrols

317
00:16:28,900 --> 00:16:30,160
and how do you think

318
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:31,100
you would like to handle that?

319
00:16:32,780 --> 00:16:33,840
If they don't have

320
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:35,400
any big ideas

321
00:16:35,400 --> 00:16:36,420
about handling that,

322
00:16:36,660 --> 00:16:37,480
we'll give them the advice

323
00:16:37,480 --> 00:16:38,080
I'm giving you

324
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:39,000
and see what happens.

325
00:16:40,240 --> 00:16:41,260
Get them thinking about

326
00:16:41,260 --> 00:16:42,680
what their experience was like

327
00:16:42,680 --> 00:16:43,560
as a younger scout,

328
00:16:44,200 --> 00:16:44,420
you know,

329
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:45,160
what they liked,

330
00:16:45,320 --> 00:16:46,220
what they didn't like,

331
00:16:46,560 --> 00:16:50,360
how they enjoyed their patrol

332
00:16:50,360 --> 00:16:52,000
or maybe didn't enjoy their patrol.

333
00:16:52,820 --> 00:16:55,160
And what are they going to do

334
00:16:55,160 --> 00:16:56,060
to make sure that

335
00:16:56,060 --> 00:16:57,800
everybody is happy

336
00:16:57,800 --> 00:16:58,900
with their new patrol?

337
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:01,220
Then go find something else

338
00:17:01,220 --> 00:17:02,280
to do for a while, Chuck.

339
00:17:02,440 --> 00:17:03,080
Leave them alone.

340
00:17:03,560 --> 00:17:04,520
And I'll bet you'll be pleased

341
00:17:04,520 --> 00:17:05,140
with the results.

342
00:17:05,340 --> 00:17:06,980
I know that the scouts will

343
00:17:06,980 --> 00:17:08,140
because they've invested

344
00:17:08,140 --> 00:17:09,140
the time into it

345
00:17:09,140 --> 00:17:10,700
and they've made the decision.

346
00:17:11,860 --> 00:17:12,620
You mentioned

347
00:17:12,620 --> 00:17:13,600
you're too cool, guy.

348
00:17:13,780 --> 00:17:14,800
I've got those guys.

349
00:17:15,160 --> 00:17:16,340
I think every troop does.

350
00:17:16,339 --> 00:17:20,679
I have some really talented scouts

351
00:17:20,679 --> 00:17:22,500
who are talented leaders

352
00:17:22,500 --> 00:17:23,439
and they know their stuff

353
00:17:23,439 --> 00:17:25,379
and sometimes they're just too cool

354
00:17:25,379 --> 00:17:26,300
for all the trappings

355
00:17:26,300 --> 00:17:26,919
of a uniform

356
00:17:26,919 --> 00:17:29,280
and being prepared

357
00:17:29,280 --> 00:17:30,260
and all that business.

358
00:17:32,820 --> 00:17:33,899
I've always had them.

359
00:17:34,260 --> 00:17:35,240
I've always had them around

360
00:17:35,240 --> 00:17:36,159
in one form or another

361
00:17:36,159 --> 00:17:38,020
for the last 25 or 26 years.

362
00:17:39,120 --> 00:17:40,760
My advice is to ignore

363
00:17:40,760 --> 00:17:41,560
their coolness.

364
00:17:42,939 --> 00:17:44,260
Ignore the affectations

365
00:17:44,260 --> 00:17:44,920
of coolness

366
00:17:44,920 --> 00:17:46,500
and just try and work

367
00:17:46,500 --> 00:17:47,200
with their talents.

368
00:17:47,340 --> 00:17:48,220
Don't try and fix them.

369
00:17:48,740 --> 00:17:49,840
They'll generally get the idea

370
00:17:49,840 --> 00:17:50,900
and they'll fix themselves.

371
00:17:51,780 --> 00:17:53,140
So, Chuck, I hope that helps

372
00:17:53,140 --> 00:17:56,480
and best luck to you

373
00:17:56,480 --> 00:17:57,740
in sorting out your troop

374
00:17:57,740 --> 00:17:58,740
into new patrols.

375
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:03,740
Time marked by a lack of troop.

376
00:18:04,960 --> 00:18:06,160
The world turns to

377
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:09,600
Mason and Scoutmaster.

378
00:18:09,600 --> 00:18:14,179
Ah, and how is the bison patrol

379
00:18:14,179 --> 00:18:15,000
this fine evening?

380
00:18:15,280 --> 00:18:16,740
Ah, it's Buffalo, isn't it?

381
00:18:16,939 --> 00:18:17,260
Where?

382
00:18:17,800 --> 00:18:19,000
No, no, Mr. Mason.

383
00:18:19,120 --> 00:18:20,379
That's the name of our patrol.

384
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:21,600
The Buffaloes?

385
00:18:22,060 --> 00:18:22,360
Oh.

386
00:18:23,459 --> 00:18:23,899
Naturally.

387
00:18:24,780 --> 00:18:25,139
Yes.

388
00:18:25,719 --> 00:18:26,639
Hey, hey, don't forget

389
00:18:26,639 --> 00:18:27,500
you're having supper

390
00:18:27,500 --> 00:18:28,860
with us tonight, right, Mr. M?

391
00:18:29,120 --> 00:18:31,040
Ah, the vaporous essences

392
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:33,040
emanating from the melange

393
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:34,300
of meat and vegetables

394
00:18:34,300 --> 00:18:35,139
you are preparing

395
00:18:35,139 --> 00:18:36,159
has sharply piqued

396
00:18:36,159 --> 00:18:37,100
my assorience.

397
00:18:37,100 --> 00:18:39,379
Yeah, okay.

398
00:18:39,899 --> 00:18:41,060
Hey, Johnny, how you doing?

399
00:18:41,080 --> 00:18:41,280
Hey.

400
00:18:41,620 --> 00:18:42,480
Wow, Mr. M.

401
00:18:42,699 --> 00:18:43,679
Hey, doesn't that stew

402
00:18:43,679 --> 00:18:44,439
smell great?

403
00:18:45,080 --> 00:18:45,520
Precisely.

404
00:18:45,639 --> 00:18:47,340
Jeez, I could eat a horse.

405
00:18:47,459 --> 00:18:48,740
Now, do I understand you

406
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:50,919
to exercise a figure of speech?

407
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:51,800
Yeah.

408
00:18:51,919 --> 00:18:53,980
Or to express the intention

409
00:18:53,980 --> 00:18:56,240
to consume an actual equine?

410
00:18:56,919 --> 00:18:57,320
Hey, fellas,

411
00:18:57,360 --> 00:18:58,459
we better build the fire up

412
00:18:58,459 --> 00:18:59,040
a little bit, huh?

413
00:19:01,040 --> 00:19:01,439
What?

414
00:19:02,919 --> 00:19:03,360
Scouts,

415
00:19:04,360 --> 00:19:06,540
I fear I must tell you

416
00:19:06,540 --> 00:19:08,000
you have fundamentally

417
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:08,980
underestimated

418
00:19:08,980 --> 00:19:10,180
the volume of fuel

419
00:19:10,180 --> 00:19:11,399
required to raise

420
00:19:11,399 --> 00:19:11,980
the temperature

421
00:19:11,980 --> 00:19:12,980
of your comestibles

422
00:19:12,980 --> 00:19:15,040
and thus achieve

423
00:19:15,040 --> 00:19:16,420
a state of optimum

424
00:19:16,420 --> 00:19:17,399
edibility.

425
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:19,320
What?

426
00:19:19,840 --> 00:19:21,020
Get some more

427
00:19:21,020 --> 00:19:21,879
sticks.

428
00:19:24,560 --> 00:19:25,720
Hey, you guys.

429
00:19:26,080 --> 00:19:26,820
The stew's ready.

430
00:19:27,720 --> 00:19:28,720
Here you go, Mr. M.

431
00:19:28,840 --> 00:19:29,540
Ah, thank you.

432
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:31,500
Ouchie, ouchie, ouchie,

433
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:32,620
ouchie, ouchie, ouchie,

434
00:19:32,620 --> 00:19:34,100
ouchie, ouch.

435
00:19:34,100 --> 00:19:34,659
Yes.

436
00:19:35,659 --> 00:19:36,879
It seems the aluminum

437
00:19:36,879 --> 00:19:38,560
alloy of my mess kit

438
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:41,320
was chosen for its ability

439
00:19:41,320 --> 00:19:42,280
to transfer heat

440
00:19:42,280 --> 00:19:43,980
to its exterior surface.

441
00:19:44,620 --> 00:19:45,120
Yeah, yeah,

442
00:19:45,199 --> 00:19:45,899
you gotta watch out.

443
00:19:45,959 --> 00:19:47,240
The stew was really hot.

444
00:19:47,399 --> 00:19:48,719
It was on the fire.

445
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:55,960
Join us again for another

446
00:19:55,960 --> 00:19:58,140
exciting episode in the career

447
00:19:58,140 --> 00:20:00,340
